According to the official DOS build 'v' key info:

PC NetHack djgpp Version 3.4.3 - last build Sun Dec  7 18:40:06 2003.


Slashem, Spork, some tournament servers, and a chaotic jumble of
patches spread across countless web sites is what we have to show for
the last 5 years of work on a still very popular game with an active
community.

Much of this is good stuff, but I'll say it again:  a non-releasing,
mostly silent official dev team hinders progress of the game and
spread and adoption of new gameplay ideas.  The official imprimatur
means something.  Nethack.org should be a focus of organization.  If
the people comprising the dev team are too busy, that's fine, but they
should step aside, or integrate, and let the official project be
developed and tested by others in the community.  There are a variety
of tools and methodologies available now to let this happen in
reasonable ways.

Please, no retorts of "do it yourself then!", because I'll ignore them
as usual.  The argument is invalid.  I'm not a developer, but there is
obviously much development talent and will in the community, and it's
not being focused to move the game forward with actual releases. There
is too much coziness with branchitis, and with the philosophy that a
good product can only be developed by a small, secretive team that
publishes work at a snail's pace.  It doesn't have to be that way.

5 years, people.

Jim


